Issue: The harness asks its second question after the answer has been written and hands it that answer: on the node this one derives from, a review panel was shown the panel's own reading beside the memory's demonstrated answer list and asked to complete it, and of 89 reviews 87 repeated the answer byte-for-byte, no addition was accepted anywhere, while the reviews were the second biggest cost in the run (11.69 model calls a sample and 43.0M input tokens on LawBench, against 4.6 calls and a whole sample of unspent budget before them). The deficit the vote cannot reach is one-directional and demonstrated: of the tip run's 43 wrong LawBench samples 31 named fewer charges than the reference, in 22 of them every missing charge is a string the frozen memory demonstrates — [[LawBench/conversations/0002/sample-0.json]] answers a three-charge case with "故意伤害" while the memory carries "聚众斗殴" and "非法持有、私藏枪支、弹药" — and the panel's own reasoning never names the charges it leaves out (4 of the 51 missing charges appear in the replies' reasoning at all). The reading the draws never get is therefore not more agreement (the vote is measured to sit at the oracle of its draws) but the memory's answer space asked as a question of its own, with nothing of the panel's answer in front of the model to repeat back.

Modification: the second reading is a recognition panel over the memory's own demonstrated answers instead of a confirmation of the panel's answer, and the answers a panel compares are read as the sets they are, in `solver/agent.py` and `solver/answer_space.py` only. `REVIEW_BLOCK` (the panel's answer plus the demonstrated list, "add only where the question clearly requires", "repeat it exactly if complete") becomes `MENU_BLOCK`: the case alone, every answer the memory demonstrates for the task, and the instruction to name the ones the facts establish, quoting the fact that establishes each. It is asked of a panel of its own — the same ceiling, wall, unanimity stop and failure tolerance — it carries no demonstration set (the menu is the evidence, so a draw is a few thousand characters instead of the task's 105K-character question), and it runs for every sample whose answer space joins several answers into one rather than only for panels that agreed with themselves. Its reading is still taken only through `extends_answer`: every answer the panel gave survives whole, everything added is a string the memory demonstrates, and the reading agreed with itself. `answers_agree` and `panel_winner` now compare answers by `answer_key`, which for a space whose demonstrated answers are joined by a separator compares the set of pieces rather than the rendering, so a panel that re-lists the same answers in another order is one answer rather than a disagreement; a space whose answers are never joined compares the whole string exactly as before. Grounding, the vote, the delivery ladder, `REFUSALS_PER_RUNG`, `PANEL_CEILING`, `PANEL_MINIMUM`, `PANEL_SETTLE`, `PANEL_FAILURES`, memory preparation and the retrieval rendering are unchanged.

Results: {"evaluation_result": {"USPTO": 0.3, "Symptom2Disease": 0.9, "LawBench": 0.59, "average": 0.5967, "status": "ok"}, "message": "The expected effect was not achieved: the mechanism ran exactly as designed on every sample it could run on and changed no answer. The menu panel ran on all 100 LawBench samples (the review ran on 89) and produced 0 accepted extensions, because the premise that an independent reading of the same case is a superset of the panel's answer is false: in the 16 samples where the menu's reading differs from the panel's, the two readings substitute answers rather than add them ([[LawBench/conversations/0017/sample-0.json]]: panel \"盗窃;非法买卖弹药、爆炸物\" against menu \"盗窃;非法持有、私藏枪支、弹药\"; [[LawBench/conversations/0037/sample-0.json]], where the menu's reading \"引诱、容留、介绍卖淫\" is the demonstrated answer and the panel's \"容留他人卖淫\" is not, and the gate still refuses it for not keeping the panel's answer whole), and the traces' own gate test finds panel ⊆ reading in 0 of 100 samples — against 0 of 89 differing in the anchored parent, so the anchor had been doing all the work and removing it made the reading independent without making it additive. The 84 samples whose readings agree are no-ops by construction. The movement is churn: LawBench 57 -> 59 with 3 samples in and 1 out, USPTO 19 -> 18 with 1 in and 2 out, Symptom2Disease 90/100 unchanged, and the 8 changed LawBench predictions sit on panels that drew the same number of times ([[LawBench/conversations/0028/sample-1.json]] flips in on the panel's own vote, not on the menu). Cost is certain and fell: model calls 1935 -> 1623, input tokens 49.5M -> 33.3M (LawBench alone 43.0M -> 26.7M), LawBench mean calls 11.69 -> 9.13, while output tokens rose 3.67M -> 4.31M because the menu writes the fact that establishes each answer. Every sample scored: 260/260, completion_rate 1.0, failed_samples 0, status ok, rankable true; USPTO and Symptom2Disease never ran a menu (their answer spaces join no several answers into one) and paid only their own churn (USPTO mean elapsed 391 -> 390s, max 666 -> 806s, inside the runner's 900s wall)."}
